<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🔄 LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 5, 2026 10:58PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 5, 2026 11:58PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (nowcast)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/surface_currents/legend.png?v=lsofs-20261005-t18z-n006-lmhofs-20261005-t18z-n006-leofs-20261006-t00z-n006-loofs-20261006-t00z-n006-hec-100600-michgan-huron-100600" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Surface-current speed (centimeters per second, one continuous gradient): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue stagnant &rarr; <b>10</b> teal &rarr; <b>20</b> yellow &rarr; <b>30</b> orange &rarr; <b>50</b> red &rarr; <b>75</b> red-violet &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 cm/s</b> dark-purple channel jets. Same speed always shows the same color; above 100 stays dark-purple. Thousands of fine white streaks RIDE the flow — each traces a short RK2 streamline of the filed U/V field (brighter streak = faster water), with tiny chevrons pointing downstream. Now: max <b>232</b>, median <b>13.2 cm/s</b>, median flow <b>191&deg; (S)</b>. NO DATA stays transparent — never zero.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NOS Great Lakes Operational Forecast System (GLOFS: LSOFS/LMHOFS/LEOFS/LOOFS) surface-current nowcast + NOAA/GLERL experimental GLCFS-FVCOM Huron-Erie Corridor & lower St. Marys River nowcast fill<br/><a href="https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html">https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 5, 2026 10:58PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 5, 2026 11:58PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</h2><p>How fast the surface water is moving right now (color = speed, brighter streaks = faster) and which way it flows (thousands of fine flow streaks with tiny downstream chevrons). Covers all five Great Lakes plus the connecting rivers and channels where NOAA models provide current vectors.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/surface_currents/legend.png?v=lsofs-20261005-t18z-n006-lmhofs-20261005-t18z-n006-leofs-20261006-t00z-n006-loofs-20261006-t00z-n006-hec-100600-michgan-huron-100600" width="600" alt="key"></p><p>Surface-current speed (centimeters per second, one continuous gradient): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue stagnant &rarr; <b>10</b> teal &rarr; <b>20</b> yellow &rarr; <b>30</b> orange &rarr; <b>50</b> red &rarr; <b>75</b> red-violet &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 cm/s</b> dark-purple channel jets. Same speed always shows the same color; above 100 stays dark-purple. Thousands of fine white streaks RIDE the flow — each traces a short RK2 streamline of the filed U/V field (brighter streak = faster water), with tiny chevrons pointing downstream. Now: max <b>232</b>, median <b>13.2 cm/s</b>, median flow <b>191&deg; (S)</b>. NO DATA stays transparent — never zero.</p><p><b>Units:</b> cm/s (speed gradient + streamlet brightness; chevrons show downstream direction)<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NOS Great Lakes Operational Forecast System (GLOFS: LSOFS/LMHOFS/LEOFS/LOOFS) surface-current nowcast + NOAA/GLERL experimental GLCFS-FVCOM Huron-Erie Corridor & lower St. Marys River nowcast fill<br/><b>Update:</b> GLOFS 6-hourly cycles + GLCFS 12-hourly, checked hourly<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html">CO-OPS THREDDS</a> + <a href="https://apps.glerl.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog/glcfs/glcfs-catalog.html">GLERL GLCFS THREDDS</a></p>]]></description>
